    expectedAnswer: "A typed groupBy implementation should infer the valid key and preserve the grouped value type.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the topic.\nA typed groupBy implementation should infer the valid key and preserve the grouped value type.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method.\nStart with one small example, identify the compile-time contract, then trace the runtime JavaScript behavior.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\n```ts\ntype Item = {\n  category: string;\n  value: number;\n};\n\nfunction groupByManual<\n  T,\n  K extends string\n>(\n  items: T[],\n  getKey: (item: T) => K,\n): Record<K, T[]> {\n  const result =\n    {} as Record<K, T[]>;\n\n  for (const item of items) {\n    const key = getKey(item);\n\n    if (!result[key]) {\n      result[key] = [];\n    }\n\n    result[key].push(item);\n  }\n\n  return result;\n}\n\nconsole.log(\n  groupByManual(\n    [\n      { category: \"a\", value: 1 },\n      { category: \"b\", value: 2 },\n      { category: \"a\", value: 3 },\n    ],\n    item => item.category,\n  ),\n);\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / IDIOMATIC TypeScript:\n```ts\nconst grouped =\n  Object.groupBy(\n    items,\n    item => item.category,\n  );\n\nconsole.log(grouped);\n```\n\nStep 5 — Example result:\n```text\n{ a: [1, 3], b: [2] }\n```\n\nStep 6 — Complexity / trade-off.\nManual version is O(n) average; Object.groupBy is concise when the runtime supports it.\n\nStep 7 — Edge cases.\nCheck null/undefined, invalid external data, inference loss, module/runtime boundaries, errors, stale async work, and API evolution where relevant.\n\nStep 8 — Senior interview takeaway.\nExplain what the compiler proves, what exists at runtime, why the idiomatic/built-in approach is useful, and the maintainability/performance trade-off.",
