    expectedAnswer: "A typed mapValues transforms every value while preserving the original object keys.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the topic.\nA typed mapValues transforms every value while preserving the original object keys.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method.\nStart with one small example, identify the compile-time contract, then trace the runtime JavaScript behavior.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\n```ts\ntype Input = {\n  a: number;\n  b: number;\n};\n\nfunction mapValuesManual<\n  T extends Record<string, unknown>,\n  R\n>(\n  input: T,\n  mapper: (value: T[keyof T]) => R,\n): Record<keyof T, R> {\n  const output =\n    {} as Record<keyof T, R>;\n\n  for (const key in input) {\n    output[key] =\n      mapper(input[key]);\n  }\n\n  return output;\n}\n\nconsole.log(\n  mapValuesManual(\n    { a: 1, b: 2 },\n    value => String(value),\n  ),\n);\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / IDIOMATIC TypeScript:\n```ts\nconst result =\n  Object.fromEntries(\n    Object.entries(input).map(\n      ([key, value]) => [\n        key,\n        String(value),\n      ],\n    ),\n  );\n```\n\nStep 5 — Example result:\n```text\n{ a: \"1\", b: \"2\" }\n```\n\nStep 6 — Complexity / trade-off.\nManual loop makes the transformation relationship explicit; Object.entries/fromEntries is concise for ordinary objects.\n\nStep 7 — Edge cases.\nCheck null/undefined, invalid external data, inference loss, module/runtime boundaries, errors, stale async work, and API evolution where relevant.\n\nStep 8 — Senior interview takeaway.\nExplain what the compiler proves, what exists at runtime, why the idiomatic/built-in approach is useful, and the maintainability/performance trade-off.",
